Note: the shared web-search budget for this turn was exhausted (0 searches were run). The evidence below comes from direct WebFetch of primary sites, plus the sources the finalist itself cited.

(1) The space is occupied by a company with the same model. MatNex (Materials Nexus, Cambridge UK; matnex.ai) says on its own homepage that it is "starting by replacing rare-earth dependence in magnets". Its stated model is that it designs and OWNS material IP, partners manufacture, and revenue comes from development fees, milestones and royalties. That is FreeMag's plan. Its backers are Ada Ventures, HTGF, Conviction Partners, Founders Factory and Cambridge Enterprise. Its news page lists:
- a £2M seed round (TechCrunch, 26 Jul 2023);
- a June 2024 Independent story: "A British firm has used AI to develop a magnet free of rare earth elements at record speed";
- a Viridien compute partnership (Feb 2025);
- a UKAEA SHINE contract (Feb 2026);
- magnetic MACE ML-potential work with Symmetric Group (Apr 2026).
So an AI-to-licence player has been running at this exact rung since 2023. Despite an announced AI-discovered magnet in 2024, its public site discloses no licence deal and no customer or manufacturing partner. That weakens the claim that a $10-100M licence buyer exists for lab-scale compositions.

(2) The data is already legible and widely screened. OQMD, Materials Project, AFLOW and Novamag are structured and API-accessible, with computed moments and anisotropy. The groups screening them include Fraunhofer IWM's MagnetPredictor (2018), Ames CMI (which has an ML-Accelerated Materials Discovery Center), GNoME/MatterGen-class generative models, and MatNex's magnetic MACE potentials. The intrinsic answer the ML would predict already sits in a legible computed field, which is the failure mode the rubric names. The illegible layer (scanned historical alloy reports) is thin and is not where the signal comes from.

(3) The bottleneck is not where ML helps. Ames's L10 FeNi highlight page discusses a large anisotropy field and a theoretical (BH)max above 40 MGOe, but reports no usable bulk coercivity. The earlier finding of 1.2 kOe in Ni-rich FeNi still stands: the real gate is extrinsic coercivity and processing, which intrinsic-property ML does not predict. Niron had to raise over $79M in equity, take a $17.5M DOE grant and a conditional $150M OSC loan, and become a manufacturer. That points to processing, not discovery, as the value step.

(4) The hit rate is low, but proving is cheap (about $2-5k per arc-melt + VSM test), so the dollar capex lever is small. Prior art from public DFT entries also weakens composition claims, so the right is first-to-file, not bought cheaply from owners who cannot read the data.
